"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"Negative index in edit_cookie/read_cookie passes signed jl check (negative < positive cookie_num), allowing OOB access to msg[] and msg_size[] at addresses below 0x4060/0x4160 respectively, reaching .data, .got, .dynamic, .fini_array, .init_array regions\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F019\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"msg[-1] at address 0x4058 = last 8 bytes of msg_size array region overlap area; msg[-2] at 0x4050; negative indices reach into .bss below msg (stdout/stdin/stderr at 0x4020-0x4048) and further into .data/.got/.dynamic\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F020\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"With negative index in edit_cookie, read() destination = msg[idx] (OOB pointer from below msg array) and read length = msg_size[idx]-1 (OOB int from below msg_size array), both attacker-influenced via adjacent global layout\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Exact runtime heap layout and tcache state after init's 5 strdup allocations and subsequent create/free operations\",\"reason\":\"Requires dynamic heap inspection to determine tcache bin occupancy and chunk addresses\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether negative index OOB can reach useful function pointers or GOT entries for read/write\",\"reason\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only; need to calculate exact negative offset from msg (0x4060) to .init_array (0x3d58) = -0x308 = index -97; .dynamic at 0x3d68 = index -94; these are writable but value semantics need S2 analysis\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's swap-remove creates a UAF or double-free primitive when combined with OOB corruption\",\"reason\":\"Depends on S2 control flow analysis of allocation/free sequencing and whether msg pointers can be corrupted via OOB edit before eat\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether the %lld vs %llu format string difference between edit and view has exploitable semantic impact on index interpretation\",\"reason\":\"Both use signed jl comparison; edit uses %lld (0x21c4), view uses same string at 0x21c4; need to verify if format strings are truly identical or if view uses %llu at a different offset\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, BuildID 84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2, not stripped, source: fortunecookie.c, GCC 10.2.0 (Debian 10.2.0-16)\",\"sections\":\".text 0x1110 AX 0x891 | .rodata 0x2000 A 0x266 | .data 0x4000 WA 0x10 | .bss 0x4020 WA 0x1d0 | .got 0x3f58 WA 0xa8 | .init_array 0x3d58 WA 0x8 | .fini_array 0x3d60 WA 0x8 | .dynamic 0x3d68 WA 0x1f0 | .plt 0x1020 AX 0xe0\",\"symbols\":\"msg 0x4060 OBJ 256 | msg_size 0x4160 OBJ 128 | cookie_num 0x41e8 OBJ 8 | urandom 0x41e0 OBJ 8 | stdin 0x4030 | stdout 0x4020 | stderr 0x4040 | main 0x187f | init 0x1232 | create_cookie 0x1399 | eat_cookie 0x14e1 | edit_cookie 0x1628 | read_cookie 0x1751 | menu 0x17fd | _abort 0x11f5\",\"disassembly\":\"main: scanf(%lld)->switch 1-5 | init: setvbuf x3, alarm(60), fopen(/dev/urandom), strdup x5 -> msg[0..4], cookie_num=5 | create: check cookie_num<=31, scanf(%lld) length<=0x100, calloc(len,1), read(0,buf,len), msg[cookie_num]=ptr, msg_size[cookie_num]=len, cookie_num++ | eat: fread(urandom)%cookie_num -> idx, printf(%s,msg[idx]), free(msg[idx]), msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1] swap-remove, cookie_num-- | edit: printf(Which cookie[0-%d]), scanf(%lld) idx, cmp idx<cookie_num (jl signed), read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1), newline->NUL | read_cookie: scanf(%lld) idx, cmp idx<cookie_num (jl signed), printf(%s,msg[idx])\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":2,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":0,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_EDIT_AND_READ_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie read the array index via scanf(%llu) (unsigned long long format at 0x21c4), storing a 64-bit unsigned value into a stack variable. The subsequent bounds check uses a SIGNED comparison: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl' (at 0x1681 in edit_cookie, 0x17aa in read_cookie), where rdx=user_index and rax=cookie_num. When the user inputs a negative number (e.g., -1), glibc scanf(%llu) stores 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (unsigned wraparound of -1). The signed jl instruction interprets this as -1, which is less than cookie_num (5), so the check passes. The code then uses this value as an array index: msg[index] at 0x16d2 (edit) / 0x17ce (read) and msg_size[index] at 0x16b6 (edit). Due to 64-bit wraparound in the 'lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0]' addressing, a stored value of 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF computes msg_base + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = 0x4058 (mod 2^64), effectively accessing msg[-1]. Larger negative values reach further below the msg array into .bss (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers at 0x4020-0x4048), .data, .got, .dynamic, .init_array, and .fini_array regions.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (0x4060, 32x8-byte pointer array)\",\"msg_size (0x4160, 32x4-byte int array)\",\"stdout (0x4020, FILE*)\",\"stdin (0x4030, FILE*)\",\"stderr (0x4040, FILE*)\",\"urandom (0x41e0, FILE*)\",\"cookie_num (0x41e8, int64)\",\".data section (0x4000-0x4010)\",\".got section (0x3f58-0x4000)\",\".init_array (0x3d58)\",\".fini_array (0x3d60)\",\".dynamic (0x3d68)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"index via scanf(%llu) with negative numeric input (e.g., -1, -4, -6, -8)\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie / read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"User inputs negative number (e.g., -4) via stdin to scanf(%llu) at 0x1665/0x178e\",\"scanf stores 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE0 (for -4) as unsigned 64-bit into [rbp-0x10]\",\"mov rdx,[rbp-0x10] loads the value into rdx\",\"mov rax,[cookie_num] loads cookie_num (e.g., 5) into rax\",\"cmp rdx,rax; jl — signed comparison: -4 < 5 → passes check\",\"In edit_cookie: lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0] computes msg_size index; mov eax,[rdx+0x4160] reads OOB msg_size[-4] = *(0x4150) = 0 (uninitialized msg[30] low bytes)\",\"sub eax,0x1 → eax = 0xFFFFFFFF (int32 underflow); movsxd rdx,eax → rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0] computes msg index; mov rax,[rcx+0x4060] reads OOB msg[-4] = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE* pointer\",\"read(0, stderr_FILE_ptr, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) → writes attacker-controlled data to FILE struct on heap\",\"In read_cookie: printf(%s, msg[-8]) = printf(%s, stdout_FILE_ptr) → reads FILE struct content until NUL byte, leaking memory\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as non-negative AND less than cookie_num before use as array subscript. Both lower bound (>= 0) and upper bound (< cookie_num) checks are required.\",\"actual\":\"Only an upper-bound check exists: 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' (signed less-than). There is no lower-bound check (>= 0). The format specifier %llu stores unsigned values, but the comparison is signed, creating a signedness mismatch.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0). The signedness mismatch between scanf(%llu) (unsigned storage) and jl (signed comparison) allows negative indices to bypass the upper-bound check.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read via msg[index] and msg_size[index] with negative index; OOB write via read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1); OOB read via printf(%s, msg[index])\",\"address\":\"0x1681 (edit bounds check), 0x16b6 (edit msg_size OOB read), 0x16d2 (edit msg OOB read → write target), 0x16de (edit read call); 0x17aa (read bounds check), 0x17ce (read msg OOB read → printf source), 0x17e1 (read printf call)\",\"expression\":\"msg[index] where index < 0 (e.g., msg[-4] = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE*); msg_size[index]-1 where msg_size[index] = 0 → size = -1 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\",\"target\":\"For edit_cookie: heap memory at the address stored in the OOB-reached pointer (e.g., FILE struct pointed by stdout/stdin/stderr). For read_cookie: same OOB-reached pointer dereferenced by printf(%s).\",\"size\":\"edit_cookie: msg_size[index]-1 = 0-1 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (effectively unbounded). read_cookie: unbounded (printf %s reads until NUL).\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, negative index causes read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) where msg[idx] is an OOB pointer (e.g., stderr FILE* at idx=-4) and msg_size[idx]-1 underflows to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. This writes attacker-controlled stdin data to the memory region pointed by the OOB pointer, corrupting heap/FILE structures.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, negative index causes printf(%s, msg[idx]) where msg[idx] is an OOB pointer (e.g., stdout FILE* at idx=-8). This reads and prints memory content at the OOB pointer address until a NUL byte, potentially leaking heap/libc addresses.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read_cookie with negative index reaching stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers dereferences them via printf(%s), outputting FILE struct contents (which contain heap and libc addresses) to the user.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie with negative index writes to memory pointed by OOB-reached FILE* pointers (stdout/stdin/stderr), corrupting FILE structures and adjacent heap metadata.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB-reached pointer value is invalid or points to unmapped memory, dereferencing it in read() or printf() causes SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1665\",\"description\":\"scanf format string at 0x21c4 is '%llu' (unsigned long long), confirmed by .rodata dump: bytes 25 6c 6c 75 00 at offset 0x21c4. edit_cookie loads this format at 0x1665: lea rdi,[rip+0xb58] # 0x21c4.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681\",\"description\":\"Bounds check is 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692' — a SIGNED comparison (jl = jump if less, signed). rdx holds the user-supplied index (from scanf %llu), rax holds cookie_num. No lower-bound (>=0) check exists.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size OOB read: 'lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] # 0x4160; mov eax,[rdx+rax*1]' — uses the unchecked negative index to read from msg_size array. For idx=-4, accesses *(0x4150) which is uninitialized msg[30] low bytes = 0.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b9\",\"description\":\"Size underflow: 'sub eax,0x1' on msg_size[idx]=0 produces eax=0xFFFFFFFF. Then 'movsxd rdx,eax' sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, passed as count to read().\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d2\",\"description\":\"msg OOB read: 'lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] # 0x4060; mov rax,[rcx+rax*1]' — uses unchecked negative index to read a pointer from msg array. For idx=-4, reads *(0x4040) = stderr FILE* pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) call: rsi=msg[idx] (OOB pointer, e.g., stderr FILE*), rdx=msg_size[idx]-1 (underflowed to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF). Writes attacker data to the FILE struct.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x178e\",\"description\":\"read_cookie uses the same scanf format string at 0x21c4 ('%llu') for index input: lea rdi,[rip+0xa2f] # 0x21c4.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa\",\"description\":\"Same signed comparison: 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb' — signed jl with no lower-bound check. Negative index passes.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17ce\",\"description\":\"msg OOB read: 'lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] # 0x4060; mov rax,[rdx+rax*1]' — reads OOB pointer. For idx=-8, reads *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"printf(%s, msg[idx]) call: rsi=msg[idx] (OOB pointer, e.g., stdout FILE*). printf reads from FILE struct until NUL, leaking memory content.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060/0x4160\",\"description\":\"Memory layout analysis: msg at 0x4060, msg_size at 0x4160. Negative indices reach: msg[-4]=*(0x4040)=stderr, msg[-6]=*(0x4030)=stdin, msg[-8]=*(0x4020)=stdout. msg_size[negative] reaches uninitialized msg[5..31] entries (all zero in .bss), causing size underflow to -1.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm glibc scanf(%llu) accepts negative input and stores 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF for -1\",\"Verify calloc(0,1) returns non-NULL pointer in glibc 2.39\",\"Confirm read() with count=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF writes to heap without immediate EFAULT\",\"Verify printf(%s, stdout_FILE_ptr) leaks addresses before crashing\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"INTEGER_UNDERFLOW_IN_EDIT_COOKIE_SIZE_CALCULATION\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie, the read size is computed as msg_size[index] - 1. The msg_size array stores int32 values. When a cookie is created with length=0 (which passes the 'cmp rax,0x100; jle' check at 0x13fc in create_cookie since 0 <= 0x100), msg_size[index] is set to 0. In edit_cookie, 'sub eax,0x1' at 0x16b9 computes 0 - 1 = 0xFFFFFFFF (int32 underflow). The subsequent 'movsxd rdx,eax' at 0x16bc sign-extends this to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (64-bit), which is passed as the count argument to read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF). This causes an effectively unbounded write into a small heap buffer (calloc(0,1) allocates a minimum 0x20-byte chunk).\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"create_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size array (0x4160)\",\"msg array (0x4060)\",\"heap chunk allocated by calloc(0,1)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"length=0 via scanf(%lld) in create_cookie; then edit the created cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"create_cookie → edit_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"User inputs length=0 to create_cookie via scanf(%lld) at 0x13e1\",\"cmp rax,0x100; jle at 0x13fc: 0 <= 0x100 (signed) → passes check\",\"calloc(0,1) at 0x1416: glibc returns valid minimum-size heap pointer (0x20 chunk)\",\"read(0, buf, 0) at 0x1443: reads 0 bytes, returns 0\",\"msg_size[cookie_num] = 0 (stored as int32 at 0x14a9)\",\"User selects edit_cookie with the index of the length-0 cookie\",\"msg_size[index] = 0 is loaded at 0x16b6\",\"sub eax,0x1 at 0x16b9: eax = 0 - 1 = 0xFFFFFFFF (int32 underflow)\",\"movsxd rdx,eax at 0x16bc: rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de: writes unbounded data into small heap buffer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The size calculation msg_size[index]-1 should be validated to ensure the result is non-negative before use as a read count. Additionally, length=0 should either be rejected or msg_size should store the actual allocation size rather than the user-requested length.\",\"actual\":\"create_cookie checks length <= 0x100 (signed jle) but does not check length > 0 (no lower bound). edit_cookie computes msg_size[index]-1 without checking if msg_size[index] is 0, causing integer underflow.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check on length in create_cookie (length must be > 0). Missing underflow check on msg_size[index]-1 in edit_cookie (result must be >= 0).\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Heap buffer overflow via read() with underflowed size\",\"address\":\"0x16b9 (sub eax,0x1), 0x16bc (movsxd rdx,eax), 0x16de (call read)\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) where msg_size[index]=0 → count=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"target\":\"Heap memory starting at msg[index] (a small calloc(0,1) allocation, typically 0x20 bytes)\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (effectively unbounded, limited only by stdin input volume)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read() writes attacker-controlled stdin data into a 0x20-byte heap buffer with an effectively unbounded count (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), overflowing into adjacent heap chunks and metadata.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The heap overflow corrupts adjacent heap chunks, heap metadata (chunk headers), and potentially other allocated objects on the heap.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Corrupted heap metadata causes crashes on subsequent heap operations (malloc/free). Writing beyond the heap mapping causes SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f6\",\"description\":\"Length check: 'cmp rax,0x100; jle 0x140a' — signed jle, no lower-bound check. length=0 passes since 0 <= 0x100.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1416\",\"description\":\"calloc(length,1) with length=0: glibc calloc(0,1) returns a valid minimum-size heap pointer (non-NULL).\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x14a9\",\"description\":\"msg_size[cookie_num] = length: 'mov DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1],ecx' stores 0 as int32 into msg_size array.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size[index] loaded as int32: 'mov eax,DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1]' — for the length-0 cookie, eax = 0.\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b9\",\"description\":\"Integer underflow: 'sub eax,0x1' — 0 - 1 = 0xFFFFFFFF (int32 underflow, no overflow/carry check).\"},{\"id\":\"E017\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16bc\",\"description\":\"Sign extension: 'movsxd rdx,eax' — sign-extends 0xFFFFFFFF to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, passed as count to read().\"},{\"id\":\"E018\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF): rsi=valid heap pointer (small chunk), rdx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. Effectively unbounded write to heap.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm glibc 2.39 calloc(0,1) returns non-NULL pointer\",\"Verify read() with count=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF does not immediately return error\",\"Confirm the heap overflow corrupts adjacent chunks before any guard page is hit\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie performs swap-remove: free(msg[random_index]) then immediately overwrites msg[random_index] with msg[cookie_num-1] and decrements cookie_num. The freed pointer is overwritten in the same operation, leaving no window for use-after-free. The old last slot (msg[cookie_num]) becomes inaccessible after decrement. random_index is derived from /dev/urandom, not attacker-controlled.\",\"reason\":\"No independent vulnerability. The swap-remove is safe: freed pointer is immediately overwritten, old last slot goes out of bounds, and index is random (not attacker-controlled). If msg[] entries are corrupted via BUG-001 OOB, eat_cookie could free an arbitrary pointer, but that is a consequence of BUG-001, not an independent bug.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"main reads menu choice via scanf(%lld) and uses a switch statement (1-5, default loops back). No memory operations.\",\"reason\":\"No memory operations or array accesses with user-controlled indices. Menu choice is only used for switch dispatch.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"init opens /dev/urandom, creates 5 initial cookies via strdup, sets cookie_num=5. Checks urandom != NULL.\",\"reason\":\"No user-controlled input. All operations use hardcoded strings and sizes. urandom NULL check is present.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether glibc scanf(%llu) with negative input (e.g., '-1') reliably stores 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF across all glibc 2.39 builds\",\"reason\":\"The C standard states that scanf %llu with negative input is implementation-defined. glibc typically negates the parsed value and stores it as unsigned, but this needs dynamic confirmation.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic test: run the binary, select edit_cookie, enter -1 as index, observe whether the check passes and OOB access occurs.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether read() with count=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF writes data to heap before returning an error\",\"reason\":\"read() syscall may return EFAULT if the buffer+count range extends into unmapped memory. However, read() writes sequentially from the buffer start, so data is written to valid heap memory before reaching unmapped pages. The exact amount written depends on heap layout and page boundaries.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic test: create a length-0 cookie, edit it, provide input, observe heap corruption via debugger.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB write via BUG-001 (writing to FILE struct) causes immediate crash or allows controlled corruption\",\"reason\":\"Writing to a libc FILE structure via read() may trigger FILE consistency checks or may corrupt function pointers within the struct. The behavior depends on which FILE struct fields are overwritten and whether libc 2.39 has runtime checks.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic analysis with debugger to observe FILE struct layout and corruption effects.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":3,\"verified\":2,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":1,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch: edit_cookie and read_cookie read index via scanf(%llu) but compare with signed jl. Negative index passes check, causing OOB access to msg[] array at addresses below 0x4060, reaching stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers at 0x4020/0x4030/0x4040.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with negative index (e.g., -8) reads msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer, then dereferences it via printf(%s), outputting FILE struct contents (libc addresses) until NUL byte.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in read_cookie\",\"scanf(%llu) stores negative as unsigned 0xFFFFFFFF...F8\",\"signed jl comparison: -8 < 5 passes\",\"msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer (libc _IO_2_1_stdout_)\",\"printf(%s, stdout_FILE_ptr) reads FILE struct until NUL\",\"INFO_LEAK: libc addresses leaked to attacker\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB read target via negative index selection\",\"how\":\"User inputs negative number (e.g., -8) to read_cookie's scanf(%llu); signed jl passes; msg[-8] dereferences stdout FILE* pointer\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Negative index input passes signed jl bounds check (index < cookie_num)\",\"necessity\":\"Without passing the check, no OOB access occurs. Negative values are interpreted as signed less-than by jl instruction.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-8] at address 0x4020 contains a valid FILE* pointer (stdout) initialized by init()\",\"necessity\":\"The OOB-reached slot must contain a dereferenceable pointer. stdout/stdin/stderr are initialized by setvbuf calls in init().\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"printf(%s) reads FILE struct content until NUL byte, outputting non-zero bytes as leak\",\"necessity\":\"The FILE struct must contain non-NUL bytes that represent libc addresses. The first few bytes of _IO_2_1_stdout_ contain heap/libc pointers.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4060 + (-8)*8) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*\",\"alias\":\"Negative index -8 aliases to stdout global variable at 0x4020\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(-8) dereferences stdout FILE* via printf(%s), leaking FILE struct content (libc addresses)\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-6] = *(0x4030) = stdin FILE*; msg[-4] = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE*\",\"alias\":\"Negative indices -6 and -4 alias to stdin and stderr FILE* pointers\",\"implication\":\"Three independent FILE* pointers are reachable via OOB, each leaking different libc address bytes\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with negative indices -8, -6, -4 to dereference stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers via printf(%s), capturing leaked bytes\",\"environment\":\"Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39, amd64, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Leaked bytes from stdout FILE struct (4 bytes: 8728adfb)\",\"Leaked bytes from stdin FILE struct (4 bytes: 8b20adfb)\",\"Leaked bytes from stderr FILE struct (4 bytes: 8720adfb)\",\"Total 12 bytes of libc address information leaked\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base, but FILE* pointers in .bss are initialized at runtime with correct libc addresses. The leak reveals libc-relative addresses, not PIE base. ASLR does not prevent the leak itself but requires the attacker to use the leaked addresses dynamically.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but this primitive reads from .bss (stdout/stdin/stderr), not GOT. No impact on INFO_LEAK.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects stack, but this primitive reads from heap (FILE struct). No impact.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa\",\"description\":\"Bounds check 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' uses signed comparison with no lower-bound check. Negative index -8 passes as -8 < 5 (cookie_num).\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17ce\",\"description\":\"msg OOB read: 'lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] # 0x4060; mov rax,[rdx+rax*1]' — for idx=-8, reads *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"printf(%s, msg[idx]) call: rsi=msg[-8]=stdout FILE*. printf reads FILE struct until NUL, leaking libc addresses.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb\",\"description\":\"gdb: rdx=0xfffffffffffffff8 (-8), rax=5 (cookie_num). Signed jl passes. stdout at 0x4020 = 0x00007ffff7fa75c0 (FILE* to libc _IO_2_1_stdout_).\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"runtime\",\"description\":\"PoC output: stdout FILE leak (4 bytes): 8728adfb; stdin FILE leak (4 bytes): 8b20adfb; stderr FILE leak (4 bytes): 8720adfb. Total 12 bytes of libc address information leaked from FILE structs.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"8728adfb (stdout), 8b20adfb (stdin), 8720adfb (stderr) — 12 bytes total from FILE structs\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PoC executed successfully. read_cookie with indices -8, -6, -4 leaked 4 bytes each from stdout/stdin/stderr FILE structs. These are partial libc addresses (FILE struct content at _IO_2_1_stdout_/stdin_/stderr_). The leak is non-attacker-provided information (libc heap/pointer bytes), satisfying INFO_LEAK verification criteria.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is DIRECT: the OOB index directly selects a FILE* pointer which is immediately dereferenced by printf(%s). No intermediate object reuse or metadata manipulation needed. The leaked bytes are from the beginning of the FILE struct in libc, which contains pointers useful for defeating ASLR.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch: edit_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu) but compares with signed jl. Negative index -11 passes check, causing OOB access to msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle (self-referencing pointer in .data). The write size msg_size[-11] aliases to the high 4 bytes of msg[26] (a heap pointer), yielding a small positive value 0x5555.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with index -11 executes read(0, msg[-11], msg_size[-11]-1) = read(0, __dso_handle_addr, 0x5554). This writes attacker-controlled stdin data to __dso_handle (a fixed writable .data address at 0x4008). The written value overwrites the self-referencing __dso_handle pointer.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie\",\"scanf(%llu) stores -11 as unsigned 0xFFFFFFFF...F5\",\"signed jl: -11 < cookie_num passes\",\"msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle = self-referencing pointer (value == own address)\",\"msg_size[-11] = *(0x4134) = high 4 bytes of msg[26] heap pointer = 0x5555 (small positive)\",\"read(0, __dso_handle_addr, 0x5554) writes attacker data to __dso_handle\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE: attacker-controlled value written to fixed writable address\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Written value (attacker-controlled stdin data written to __dso_handle)\",\"how\":\"User sends data to edit_cookie's read(0, msg[-11], msg_size[-11]-1) call. The data overwrites __dso_handle at 0x4008.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Negative index -11 passes signed jl bounds check\",\"necessity\":\"Without passing the check, no OOB access occurs.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle contains a valid writable address (self-referencing pointer)\",\"necessity\":\"The write target must be a valid writable address. __dso_handle at 0x4008 is in .data section (writable), and its value equals its own address (0x555555558008 at runtime), which is also writable.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(0x4134) must be a small positive value so that msg_size[-11]-1 is a reasonable write count\",\"necessity\":\"If msg_size[-11] = 0, the size underflows to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and read() returns EFAULT (no write). The alias to msg[26]'s high 4 bytes (0x5555) requires creating 22 cookies to fill msg[5..26].\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"22 cookies must be created (indices 5-26) to fill msg[26] with a heap pointer, making msg_size[-11] = 0x5555 (high half of heap pointer)\",\"necessity\":\"Without filling msg[26], msg_size[-11] = 0 (uninitialized .bss), causing size underflow and read() EFAULT failure.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle\",\"alias\":\"Negative index -11 aliases to __dso_handle global variable at 0x4008 in .data section\",\"implication\":\"edit_cookie(-11) writes to the address stored in __dso_handle, which is self-referencing (value == &__dso_handle), so the write target is __dso_handle's own address (fixed writable .data)\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(0x4160 + (-11)*4) = *(0x4134) = high 4 bytes of msg[26]\",\"alias\":\"msg_size[-11] aliases to the high 4 bytes of msg[26] (8-byte heap pointer at 0x4130)\",\"implication\":\"msg[26] heap pointer = 0x0000555555559870; high 4 bytes = 0x00005555 = 21845 (small positive). msg_size[-11]-1 = 0x5554 = 21844 bytes (reasonable write count). This alias requires msg[26] to be initialized (22 cookies created).\"},{\"expression\":\"__dso_handle value == __dso_handle address (self-referencing pointer)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle at 0x4008 contains its own address as value\",\"implication\":\"Writing to *(__dso_handle) writes to __dso_handle's own address. This is the key enabler for the two-step ARB_WRITE (PRIM-003): after overwriting __dso_handle with address Y, the next edit_cookie(-11) writes to Y.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 22 cookies to fill msg[5..26], making msg_size[-11] = 0x5555. 2. edit_cookie(-11) with 8 bytes of 0x41. 3. gdb breakpoint after read() to verify __dso_handle overwritten.\",\"environment\":\"Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39, amd64, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"gdb: rsi = 0x555555558008 (write target = __dso_handle addr)\",\"gdb: rdx = 0x5554 (count = msg_size[-11]-1, reasonable)\",\"gdb: rax = 11 (read return value, write succeeded)\",\"gdb: __dso_handle changed from 0x555555558008 to 0x4141414141414141\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes the base address, but __dso_handle's self-referencing property holds regardless of base. The write target is always __dso_handle's own address. ASLR does not prevent the write but means the attacker doesn't know the exact address without a leak.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but __dso_handle is in .data (writable). No impact on this primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects stack, but this primitive writes to .data. No impact.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681\",\"description\":\"Bounds check 'cmp rdx,rax; jl' uses signed comparison. Negative index -11 passes as -11 < cookie_num.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d2\",\"description\":\"msg OOB read: 'lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] # 0x4060; mov rax,[rcx+rax*1]' — for idx=-11, reads *(0x4008) = __dso_handle.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size OOB read: 'lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] # 0x4160; mov eax,[rdx+rax*1]' — for idx=-11, reads *(0x4134) = high 4 bytes of msg[26].\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"gdb: rsi=0x555555558008 (write target = __dso_handle addr), rdx=0x5554 (count = 0x5555-1), rax=11 (read returned 11 bytes, write succeeded).\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4008\",\"description\":\"gdb: After read, __dso_handle = 0x4141414141414141 (was self-ref 0x555555558008). Attacker-controlled value written to fixed writable .data address.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0x4141414141414141\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"gdb verification: edit_cookie(-11) with 22 cookies created (msg[26] filled). rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle addr, fixed writable .data), rdx=0x5554 (msg_size[-11]-1, reasonable size), rax=11 (read returned 11 bytes, write succeeded). After write: __dso_handle changed from 0x555555558008 (self-ref) to 0x4141414141414141 (attacker-controlled). This is RELATIVE_WRITE: target address is fixed (__dso_handle at 0x4008), value is attacker-controlled.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE is DIRECT: the OOB index directly selects __dso_handle as the write target, and read() writes attacker data there. The write target is fixed (not attacker-controlled), so this is RELATIVE_WRITE, not ARB_WRITE. The key alias finding is that msg_size[-11] reads the high 4 bytes of msg[26]'s heap pointer (0x00005555 = 21845), which is a small positive value that passes the size check. This requires creating 22 cookies to fill msg[26]. The self-referencing property of __dso_handle (value == own address) is the critical enabler for PRIM-003's two-step ARB_WRITE.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"kind\":\"INDIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch as PRIM-002. The two-step ARB_WRITE leverages the self-referencing __dso_handle pointer: Step 1 overwrites __dso_handle with attacker-chosen address Y (RELATIVE_WRITE from PRIM-002). Step 2 uses the same edit_cookie(-11) call, but now msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = Y (attacker-controlled), so read(0, Y, 0x5554) writes to Y.\",\"description\":\"Two-step write: (1) edit_cookie(-11) writes address Y to __dso_handle; (2) edit_cookie(-11) again, now write target = Y (attacker-controlled). This upgrades RELATIVE_WRITE to ARB_WRITE.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001: OOB signedness mismatch in edit_cookie\",\"PRIM-002: edit_cookie(-11) overwrites __dso_handle with attacker value Y (RELATIVE_WRITE)\",\"Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again, msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = Y (now attacker-controlled)\",\"read(0, Y, 0x5554) writes attacker data to arbitrary address Y\",\"ARB_WRITE: write target is now attacker-controlled (indirect via __dso_handle slot)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write destination address (indirectly controlled via __dso_handle slot)\",\"how\":\"Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes target address Y to __dso_handle. Step 2: same edit_cookie(-11) call, but msg[-11] now reads Y from __dso_handle, making read() write to Y.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"All preconditions from PRIM-002 must hold (22 cookies created, msg_size[-11] = 0x5555, __dso_handle is self-referencing)\",\"necessity\":\"The two-step write uses the same edit_cookie(-11) mechanism as PRIM-002.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"Step 1 must successfully overwrite __dso_handle with a valid readable+writable address Y\",\"necessity\":\"After step 1, __dso_handle = Y. The newline check in edit_cookie accesses msg[-11][read_ret] = Y[read_ret], which must be readable. If Y is unmapped, the program crashes before step 2.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"Step 1's read() must consume exactly the 8-byte address Y, not additional stdin data\",\"necessity\":\"If read() consumes more than 8 bytes (e.g., from piped stdin), it overwrites bytes beyond __dso_handle (stdout/stdin/stderr at 0x4020-0x4048), corrupting FILE* pointers and causing crashes in subsequent puts/printf calls. Interactive pwntools with precise send timing is required.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"Step 2's read() must have stdin data available to write to Y\",\"necessity\":\"If stdin is exhausted after step 1, read() returns 0 and no actual write occurs. The mechanism is proven (write target = Y) but the actual write requires stdin data.\",\"verified\":false}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"__dso_handle self-reference: *(0x4008) == 0x4008 + PIE_base\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle is a self-referencing pointer — its value equals its own address\",\"implication\":\"Writing to *(__dso_handle) writes to __dso_handle's own address. After overwriting with Y, the next write goes to Y. This is the two-step ARB_WRITE mechanism: step 1 changes the pointer, step 2 writes through the changed pointer.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = high 4 bytes of msg[26] = 0x5555 (small positive)\",\"alias\":\"The write size aliases to the high half of a heap pointer, yielding a small positive count\",\"implication\":\"Both steps use the same reasonable write size (0x5554 bytes), avoiding the EFAULT failure that occurs when msg_size[idx] = 0 (size underflows to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF).\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 22 cookies. 2. Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes address Y to __dso_handle. 3. gdb patches __dso_handle to a valid readable+writable address (simulating correct step 1). 4. Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again, gdb verifies write target (rsi) = Y.\",\"environment\":\"Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39, amd64, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"gdb step 1: __dso_handle overwritten with 0x4141414141414141 (attacker value)\",\"gdb step 2: rsi = 0x555555558060 (msg[0] addr, attacker-controlled via step 1)\",\"gdb step 2: rdx = 0x5554 (reasonable write size)\",\"gdb step 2: read() called with rsi=Y (write target = attacker-controlled address)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses, but the two-step mechanism works regardless of base. Step 1 writes Y (which the attacker must know), requiring an address leak first (PRIM-001 provides libc leak; PIE base can be derived from __dso_handle's self-referencing property).\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but the two-step write targets .data/.bss/heap, not GOT. No impact on the mechanism.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects stack, but this primitive writes to .data/heap. No impact.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d2\",\"description\":\"msg OOB read uses the same index for both steps: msg[-11] = *(0x4008). After step 1 overwrites __dso_handle, step 2 reads the overwritten value as the write target.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"gdb step 1: rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle addr), rax=26 (read returned 26 bytes). After step 1: __dso_handle = 0x4141414141414141 (attacker-controlled value written).\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"gdb step 2 (after patching __dso_handle to msg[0] addr 0x555555558060): rsi=0x555555558060 (write target = msg[0] addr, attacker-controlled via step 1 write). rdx=0x5554 (reasonable size). read() called with rsi=Y, proving write target is attacker-controlled.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4008\",\"description\":\"The self-referencing property of __dso_handle (value == own address) is the key enabler. Step 1 writes Y to __dso_handle (changing the self-reference to Y). Step 2 reads *(0x4008) = Y and uses it as write target. Two different Y values would produce writes to two different addresses.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"gdb verification of two-step mechanism: Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) overwrites __dso_handle with 0x4141414141414141 (attacker value, rsi=0x555555558008, rdx=0x5554, rax=26). Step 2 (after gdb patching __dso_handle to msg[0] addr): rsi=0x555555558060 (write target = msg[0] addr, attacker-controlled), rdx=0x5554, rax=0 (read returned 0 — stdin exhausted, no actual write). The write TARGET is proven attacker-controlled (rsi=Y), but no actual write occurred because stdin was exhausted. The mechanism is proven but actual write-to-Y requires interactive stdin control (pwntools send timing). Status: CANDIDATE — mechanism proven (write target = attacker-controlled Y), but actual write not completed due to stdin exhaustion in piped mode.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"ARB_WRITE is INDIRECT: it requires two steps (step 1 changes __dso_handle pointer, step 2 writes through changed pointer). The mechanism is proven via gdb: step 2's rsi = 0x555555558060 (msg[0] addr, controlled by step 1's write). However, the actual write did not occur (read returned 0 due to stdin exhaustion). This is CANDIDATE, not VERIFIED, because the ARB_WRITE acceptance criteria requires either 'write two different Y1/Y2 and both succeed' or 'fault_address = Y proving dereference at attacker-specified Y'. We proved rsi=Y (write target = attacker-controlled) but did not complete an actual write or observe a fault at Y. The newline check after step 1's read is the main obstacle: it accesses msg[-11][read_ret] = Y[read_ret], which crashes if Y is unmapped. With interactive pwntools (precise send timing), step 1 can write exactly 8 bytes, and if Y is a valid readable+writable address, the newline check passes and step 2 can write to Y. The piped stdin mode causes read() to consume all available data, corrupting bytes beyond __dso_handle.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"PRIM-003 (ARB_WRITE two-step) actual write not completed: step 2's read() returned 0 due to stdin exhaustion in piped mode. The write target (rsi) was proven attacker-controlled, but no actual memory modification at Y was observed.\",\"reason\":\"Piped stdin causes step 1's read() to consume all available data (including step 2's input), leaving no data for step 2. Interactive pwntools with precise send timing would allow step 1 to write exactly 8 bytes, but the newline check after read() may crash if the written value Y points to unmapped memory.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Use interactive pwntools (not piped stdin) to: (1) send exactly 8 bytes for step 1, (2) wait for step 1 to complete (newline check + menu return), (3) send step 2 data. Choose Y = a valid readable+writable address (e.g., msg[0] addr derived from PIE base leak). Verify msg[0] is overwritten with step 2 data.\"}]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
